## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with non-classical truth in a constrained system, using backtracking under Kleene semantics and additional operators (↔, ⊕) to reveal subtle propagation patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics and derived operators
- Backtracking with constraint propagation
- Handling composite operators like biconditional and XOR under 3-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function imp(a,b){ // a→b = (¬a) ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return (na==='T' || b==='T') ? 'T' : (na==='U' || b==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
}
function orb(a,b){ // max in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b;
}
function land(a,b){ // min in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of temporal, multi-step constraints in a 3-valued logic setting. Requires constructing a concrete model and outlining a backtracking propagator that preserves non-F constraints while allowing Unknowns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics for implication
- Temporal constraint propagation across time steps
- Unit/branching propagation in backtracking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluateImp(A,B) returns Kleene(A,B)
function evaluateImp(A,B){ // A,B in {T,F,U}
  // ¬A ∨ B with Kleene ordering T>U>F
  const not = {T:'F', F:'T', U:'U'}[A];
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  // ⩒ is max by value order
  const values = [not, B];
  let best = 'F';
  for (const v of values) if (order[v] > order[best]) best = v;
  return best;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

